1. Introduction
   A Perl script that takes a sequence s1 (may be multiple sequences)
   in FASTA format with and outputs a file with an extension of 'pcount'
   that produces percentages of occurrences of the residues of the sequence
   in the following form:
   
   >sequence identifier
   A: 0.245
   T: 0.245
   G: 0.255
   C: 0.255
   

2. Running Environment
   Tested on windows XP and UNIX with Perl 5.8.0


3. Features
   A. Input File
       a. File name must be provided as an commandline argument
       b. File should be in a FASTA format with proper preamble 
          (description or ID) lines.
       c. More than one sequences are allowed in a file, 
          and each sequence will produce its own 
          probabilities of its characters separately.
   B. Sequence Check
       a. This script distinguish the input sequence is 
          DNA, protein, or errorneous sequences. This is done by
          checking non DNA character and non amino acid character.           

4. Options
   * Options are case-insensitive and double dash can be used as well
   -d      :      Silent option. If this is used, this script will not
                  print out any message on the screen. But a result file
                  is still going to be produced.
                  (This may be useful when this script is used by cgi program)
   * Note : File name should be provided as a command line argument
                  
5. Sample Usages
     >./find_percent.pl seq1
       - Output file is 'seq1.pcount'

     >./find_percent.pl /tmp/juhur/seq1
       - input file is located at '/tmp/juhur/' directory
       - Output file is still 'seq1.pcount' but will be created
         into '/tmp/juhur' directory
       
     >./find_percent.pl seq1 -d
       - Output file is 'seq1.pcount'
       - No screen display

6. Exception(Error)
   A. when no input sequence file specified
   B. when the specified input file does not exist in the same directory
      ( or in the specified directory )
   C. when the specified input file contains errornous sequences
      
   